{"name":"Double Fudge","hex":"#6d544b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"double","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made up of two matching or complementary elements.","Of twice the quantity."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Twice over; twofold; doubly.","Two together; two at a time."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/double"},{"term":"fudge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of very sweet candy or confection, usually made from sugar, butter, and milk or cream.","Chocolate fudge."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To try to avoid giving a direct answer.","To alter something from its true state, as to hide a flaw or uncertainty, deliberately but not necessarily dishonestly or immorally."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fudge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Double Fudge","blocks":[{"text":"Double Fudge is a 2002 children's novel by Judy Blume and the fifth and final entry in the Fudge series. The Hatcher family goes to Washington, D.C. where they spend time with their extended family, and Fudge finds out that his cousin is also named Farley Drexel Hatcher. His interest in money is a common theme throughout the story."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8b/Double_Fudge_book_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_Fudge"},{"title":"Fudge","blocks":[{"text":"Fudge is a confection made by mixing sugar, butter, and milk. Modern fudge has its origins in the 19th century United States, and became popular in American women's colleges late in the century. Fudge can come in a variety of flavorings depending on the region or country it was made; popular flavors include fruit, nut, chocolate, and caramel. Fudge is often bought as a gift from a gift shop in tourist areas and attractions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Handmade_fruit_fudge_squares.jp